It builds on work by others, whose licences it keeps:

  * quiddity  — manufacturing feature recognition from STEP B-rep models,
    Copyright (c) the quiddity authors, Apache License 2.0
    (https://github.com/pzfreo/quiddity). Used as a dependency; not modified.
  * haecceity — the exact B-rep reader and geometry kernel under quiddity, same
    project and licence. Used as a dependency; not modified.
  * ExTbpMonty.Core — the Thousand Brains Project's sensorimotor framework,
    ported to the BEAM (https://github.com/d-led/ex.tbp.monty), which is the
    project's own reimplementation of Monty (MIT, Copyright (c) Thousand Brains
    Project). `Quiddity.Percept` and `Quiddity.Monty.*` call its feature
    extraction rather than re-implementing it.

The STEP test fixtures under `test/fixtures/` came from quiddity's own test
suite, which takes them from public sources (NIST MBE PMI test cases, the
FreeCAD parts library, build123d examples); each file names its source beside it.
